1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,082
<font color="#FFFFFF">Semidefinite programming is a powerful tool for
combinatorial optimization,</font>

2
00:00:05,162 --> 00:00:09,620
<font color="#FFFFFF">but it has long been considered too expensive to
run at real-world scale.</font>

3
00:00:09,700 --> 00:00:13,910
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper introduces USBS, Unified Spectral
Bundling with Sketching,</font>

4
00:00:13,990 --> 00:00:18,945
<font color="#FFFFFF">a provably correct solver that is fast, scales to
billions of decision variables,</font>

5
00:00:19,025 --> 00:00:22,799
<font color="#FFFFFF">and, crucially, can reuse a previous solution as a
warm start.</font>

6
00:00:22,879 --> 00:00:26,094
<font color="#FFFFFF">On a MaxCut instance with over two billion variables,</font>

7
00:00:26,174 --> 00:00:30,943
<font color="#FFFFFF">USBS delivers a five-hundred-times speedup over the
previous state of the art,</font>

8
00:00:31,023 --> 00:00:36,040
<font color="#FFFFFF">and warm-starting alone can accelerate convergence
by more than one hundred times.</font>

9
00:00:36,420 --> 00:00:39,702
<font color="#FFFFFF">Semidefinite programs can model an enormous range
of practical problems,</font>

10
00:00:39,782 --> 00:00:43,063
<font color="#FFFFFF">from combinatorial optimization to neural network
verification and control.</font>

11
00:00:43,143 --> 00:00:46,425
<font color="#FFFFFF">But solving them at scale is hard.</font>

12
00:00:46,505 --> 00:00:49,786
<font color="#FFFFFF">The classic approaches require projecting onto the
semidefinite cone,</font>

13
00:00:49,866 --> 00:00:53,148
<font color="#FFFFFF">which needs a full eigendecomposition that scales
cubically with problem size.</font>

14
00:00:53,228 --> 00:00:56,509
<font color="#FFFFFF">Recent sketching methods, like CGAL,</font>

15
00:00:56,589 --> 00:00:59,871
<font color="#FFFFFF">avoid storing the full matrix and scale much further,</font>

16
00:00:59,951 --> 00:01:03,232
<font color="#FFFFFF">but they pay for it: as the problem grows they need
more and more iterations,</font>

17
00:01:03,312 --> 00:01:06,594
<font color="#FFFFFF">so convergence slows down.</font>

18
00:01:06,674 --> 00:01:09,955
<font color="#FFFFFF">Worse,</font>

19
00:01:10,035 --> 00:01:13,317
<font color="#FFFFFF">they rely on iteration-dependent parameter
schedules that prevent them from reliably reusing</font>

20
00:01:13,397 --> 00:01:16,678
<font color="#FFFFFF">a previous solution as a warm start,</font>

21
00:01:16,758 --> 00:01:20,040
<font color="#FFFFFF">which is exactly what many real applications need.</font>

22
00:01:20,420 --> 00:01:23,810
<font color="#FFFFFF">In practice, you rarely solve a single SDP in isolation.</font>

23
00:01:23,890 --> 00:01:25,483
<font color="#FFFFFF">Data arrives incrementally,</font>

24
00:01:25,563 --> 00:01:31,555
<font color="#FFFFFF">or you solve a sequence of tightly-related
subproblems inside a mixed-integer or interactive loop.</font>

25
00:01:31,635 --> 00:01:36,017
<font color="#FFFFFF">In all these settings, each new problem is nearly
identical to the last,</font>

26
00:01:36,097 --> 00:01:41,098
<font color="#FFFFFF">so being able to warm-start from the previous
solution should give a huge speedup.</font>

27
00:01:41,178 --> 00:01:46,613
<font color="#FFFFFF">Spectral bundle methods are an appealing framework
here: they have low per-iteration cost</font>

28
00:01:46,693 --> 00:01:48,534
<font color="#FFFFFF">and fast empirical convergence.</font>

29
00:01:48,614 --> 00:01:55,288
<font color="#FFFFFF">But previous spectral bundle methods handled either
only equality constraints or only inequality constraints,</font>

30
00:01:55,368 --> 00:02:01,360
<font color="#FFFFFF">and none had an efficient standalone implementation
that could be evaluated on truly massive SDPs.</font>

31
00:02:01,740 --> 00:02:05,289
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper presents USBS, a unified spectral bundle
method with sketching.</font>

32
00:02:05,369 --> 00:02:08,918
<font color="#FFFFFF">It makes several contributions.</font>

33
00:02:08,998 --> 00:02:12,548
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, it handles general SDPs with both equality
and inequality constraints,</font>

34
00:02:12,628 --> 00:02:16,177
<font color="#FFFFFF">unlike prior spectral bundle methods.</font>

35
00:02:16,257 --> 00:02:19,806
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second,</font>

36
00:02:19,886 --> 00:02:23,435
<font color="#FFFFFF">it can be augmented with an optional matrix
sketching technique that dramatically improves</font>

37
00:02:23,515 --> 00:02:27,065
<font color="#FFFFFF">scalability while keeping convergence fast.</font>

38
00:02:27,145 --> 00:02:30,694
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third, it reliably leverages warm-start initializations.</font>

39
00:02:30,774 --> 00:02:34,323
<font color="#FFFFFF">The method comes with provable non-asymptotic
convergence guarantees,</font>

40
00:02:34,403 --> 00:02:37,952
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it exposes parameters that let the user trade
off per-iteration cost against convergence speed.</font>

41
00:02:38,032 --> 00:02:41,582
<font color="#FFFFFF">Finally,</font>

42
00:02:41,662 --> 00:02:45,211
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors release a standalone implementation in
pure JAX that runs efficiently on CPUs,</font>

43
00:02:45,291 --> 00:02:48,840
<font color="#FFFFFF">GPUs, and TPUs.</font>

44
00:02:49,220 --> 00:02:50,544
<font color="#FFFFFF">Here is how USBS works.</font>

45
00:02:50,624 --> 00:02:56,464
<font color="#FFFFFF">It starts from the dual semidefinite program and
rewrites it as a single unconstrained objective,</font>

46
00:02:56,544 --> 00:03:01,774
<font color="#FFFFFF">a penalized dual that combines the largest
eigenvalue of C minus A-star y with a linear</font>

47
00:03:01,854 --> 00:03:05,008
<font color="#FFFFFF">term and an indicator for the inequality constraints.</font>

48
00:03:05,088 --> 00:03:09,464
<font color="#FFFFFF">This penalized objective is then minimized with a
proximal bundle method.</font>

49
00:03:09,544 --> 00:03:12,454
<font color="#FFFFFF">At each iteration, instead of the full objective,</font>

50
00:03:12,534 --> 00:03:17,703
<font color="#FFFFFF">USBS builds a cheap lower model over a
low-dimensional subspace spanned by the current</font>

51
00:03:17,783 --> 00:03:22,402
<font color="#FFFFFF">maximum eigenvectors and a few directions carrying
past spectral information.</font>

52
00:03:22,482 --> 00:03:27,102
<font color="#FFFFFF">It proposes a candidate iterate by taking a
proximal step against this model,</font>

53
00:03:27,182 --> 00:03:29,055
<font color="#FFFFFF">solving a small minimax problem.</font>

54
00:03:29,135 --> 00:03:34,487
<font color="#FFFFFF">If the true objective decreases by at least a fixed
fraction of what the model predicted,</font>

55
00:03:34,567 --> 00:03:36,684
<font color="#FFFFFF">it accepts the step, a descent step;</font>

56
00:03:36,764 --> 00:03:40,285
<font color="#FFFFFF">otherwise it takes a null step but still refines
the model.</font>

57
00:03:40,365 --> 00:03:43,764
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because only a small set of eigenvectors and, optionally,</font>

58
00:03:43,844 --> 00:03:47,120
<font color="#FFFFFF">a low-rank sketch of the primal matrix are ever stored,</font>

59
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:50,050
<font color="#FFFFFF">the whole procedure scales to enormous problems.</font>

60
00:03:50,430 --> 00:03:54,615
<font color="#FFFFFF">The method is tested across three very different
application areas.</font>

61
00:03:54,695 --> 00:03:55,888
<font color="#FFFFFF">The first is MaxCut,</font>

62
00:03:55,968 --> 00:04:03,336
<font color="#FFFFFF">using ten graphs from the DIMACS10 collection
ranging from sixteen thousand up to three point seven million vertices,</font>

63
00:04:03,416 --> 00:04:07,919
<font color="#FFFFFF">the largest yielding more than ten to the
thirteenth decision variables.</font>

64
00:04:07,999 --> 00:04:10,911
<font color="#FFFFFF">The second is the quadratic assignment problem,</font>

65
00:04:10,991 --> 00:04:13,521
<font color="#FFFFFF">a notoriously hard combinatorial problem,</font>

66
00:04:13,601 --> 00:04:18,677
<font color="#FFFFFF">using instances from QAPLIB and TSPLIB with sizes
up to one hundred ninety-eight,</font>

67
00:04:18,757 --> 00:04:22,942
<font color="#FFFFFF">whose SDP relaxation has on the order of
n-to-the-fourth variables,</font>

68
00:04:23,022 --> 00:04:24,979
<font color="#FFFFFF">reaching one point five billion.</font>

69
00:04:25,059 --> 00:04:29,626
<font color="#FFFFFF">The third is interactive entity resolution using
existential constraints,</font>

70
00:04:29,706 --> 00:04:33,954
<font color="#FFFFFF">on three author-coreference datasets: PubMed, QIAN,
and SCAD-zbMATH.</font>

71
00:04:34,034 --> 00:04:38,792
<font color="#FFFFFF">In each case, warm starts are constructed naturally
from a slightly smaller,</font>

72
00:04:38,872 --> 00:04:40,320
<font color="#FFFFFF">closely related problem.</font>

73
00:04:40,700 --> 00:04:44,398
<font color="#FFFFFF">The results are striking.</font>

74
00:04:44,478 --> 00:04:48,177
<font color="#FFFFFF">On MaxCut,</font>

75
00:04:48,257 --> 00:04:51,955
<font color="#FFFFFF">USBS reaches an accurate solution on all ten
instances in twenty-eight hours or less,</font>

76
00:04:52,035 --> 00:04:55,733
<font color="#FFFFFF">even without a warm start, while the previous
state-of-the-art solver, CGAL,</font>

77
00:04:55,813 --> 00:04:59,512
<font color="#FFFFFF">fails to reach an accurate solution on seven of the
ten instances within seventy-two hours.</font>

78
00:04:59,592 --> 00:05:03,290
<font color="#FFFFFF">On an instance with over two billion decision variables,</font>

79
00:05:03,370 --> 00:05:07,068
<font color="#FFFFFF">USBS is five hundred times faster than CGAL.</font>

80
00:05:07,148 --> 00:05:10,847
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the quadratic assignment and entity-resolution tasks,</font>

81
00:05:10,927 --> 00:05:14,625
<font color="#FFFFFF">USBS reaches better relative gaps and lower
cumulative solve times,</font>

82
00:05:14,705 --> 00:05:18,403
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the gap in its favor grows as the problems get larger.</font>

83
00:05:18,483 --> 00:05:22,182
<font color="#FFFFFF">Across all three settings, USBS reliably takes
advantage of warm starts,</font>

84
00:05:22,262 --> 00:05:25,960
<font color="#FFFFFF">whereas CGAL often cannot.</font>

85
00:05:26,340 --> 00:05:28,990
<font color="#FFFFFF">Two ablations are especially informative.</font>

86
00:05:29,070 --> 00:05:31,053
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, warm-starting: for USBS,</font>

87
00:05:31,133 --> 00:05:36,979
<font color="#FFFFFF">initializing from the previous solution can speed
up convergence by more than one hundred</font>

88
00:05:37,059 --> 00:05:39,242
<font color="#FFFFFF">times compared with cold-starting,</font>

89
00:05:39,322 --> 00:05:44,568
<font color="#FFFFFF">and, importantly, USBS actually realizes this
benefit while CGAL usually cannot.</font>

90
00:05:44,648 --> 00:05:50,161
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, the model parameters: the number of current
eigenvectors, kc, matters a lot,</font>

91
00:05:50,241 --> 00:05:53,689
<font color="#FFFFFF">and larger values give better convergence in general.</font>

92
00:05:53,769 --> 00:05:58,949
<font color="#FFFFFF">This contrasts with the original spectral bundle
method, which fixed kc to one.</font>

93
00:05:59,029 --> 00:06:01,612
<font color="#FFFFFF">The number of past spectral vectors, kp,</font>

94
00:06:01,692 --> 00:06:06,538
<font color="#FFFFFF">turns out to be much less helpful and can sometimes
even harm convergence,</font>

95
00:06:06,618 --> 00:06:10,200
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the recommended settings keep kc large and kp small.</font>

96
00:06:10,580 --> 00:06:15,711
<font color="#FFFFFF">To summarize the numbers that matter most: a
five-hundred-times speedup over the previous</font>

97
00:06:15,791 --> 00:06:20,161
<font color="#FFFFFF">state of the art on a problem with more than two
billion decision variables;</font>

98
00:06:20,241 --> 00:06:24,494
<font color="#FFFFFF">more than a one-hundred-times convergence speedup
just from warm-starting;</font>

99
00:06:24,574 --> 00:06:29,003
<font color="#FFFFFF">ten out of ten MaxCut instances solved by USBS in
twenty-eight hours or less,</font>

100
00:06:29,083 --> 00:06:32,926
<font color="#FFFFFF">versus only three out of ten for CGAL even given
seventy-two hours.</font>

101
00:06:33,006 --> 00:06:37,903
<font color="#FFFFFF">USBS scales to problems with over
ten-to-the-thirteenth decision variables and solves</font>

102
00:06:37,983 --> 00:06:42,060
<font color="#FFFFFF">quadratic-assignment relaxations with one point
five billion variables.</font>

103
00:06:42,440 --> 00:06:43,819
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway is simple.</font>

104
00:06:43,899 --> 00:06:49,594
<font color="#FFFFFF">USBS shows that large-scale semidefinite
programming does not have to be slow or restricted</font>

105
00:06:49,674 --> 00:06:51,497
<font color="#FFFFFF">to a narrow class of problems.</font>

106
00:06:51,577 --> 00:06:56,764
<font color="#FFFFFF">By unifying equality and inequality constraints in
a single spectral bundle method,</font>

107
00:06:56,844 --> 00:07:01,967
<font color="#FFFFFF">adding optional sketching for scalability, and
making warm-starting actually work,</font>

108
00:07:02,047 --> 00:07:07,043
<font color="#FFFFFF">it turns SDPs that were previously considered
intractable into a practical tool,</font>

109
00:07:07,123 --> 00:07:10,850
<font color="#FFFFFF">complete with an open, hardware-flexible JAX
implementation.</font>
